---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/discount-strategy';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/discount-strategy');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/discount-strategy');
const comparisonApps = [
  'sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'drip', 'omnisend', 'privy', 'shopify-email',
  'mailchimp', 'brevo', 'yotpo-email-sms', 'rejoiner',
].map((s) => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
<a href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
<h1>Govern discounts by contribution and checkout behavior</h1>
<p>Welcome, browse, cart, and sale campaigns need one offer policy. A high attributed-revenue total can conceal incentives paid to customers who would have bought anyway. The workflow here is a proposed governance process, with hypothetical arithmetic rather than reported merchant results.</p>
<h2>Inventory the offers customers can actually use</h2><p>List popup codes, automatic discounts, lifecycle codes, loyalty rewards, shipping offers, and agency campaigns. Record the owner, eligible products and customers, minimum spend, market, expiry timezone, usage limit, and combination settings. Include codes still circulating outside your current calendar. An email suppression rule does not deactivate a leaked code at checkout.</p>
<h2>Define a contribution floor before the incentive</h2><p>Use net merchandise receipts after discount, then subtract product cost, variable fulfillment, payment cost, shipping subsidy, expected returns, and messaging costs. Keep tax and shipping accounting consistent with finance. A percentage cap alone cannot protect low-margin products or costly delivery zones.</p>
<p>Hypothetical simplified order: $100 merchandise less a $10 discount leaves $90. Subtract $40 product cost, $8 fulfillment, $3 payment cost, $5 shipping subsidy, $4 expected return cost, and $1 messaging allocation: contribution is $29. These are invented inputs for planning, not app prices or store results. Change the assumptions before using the example for a real decision.</p>
<h2>Test combinations in checkout</h2><p><a href="https://help.shopify.com/en/manual/discounts/discount-combinations">Shopify documents eligible discount combinations</a> for both codes and automatic discounts. Combination behavior depends on discount classes, settings, and eligibility. Do not assume automatic discounts always block codes or that every pair stacks.</p>
<p>Proposed checks: popup code plus sale discount; shipping reward plus product discount; excluded item mixed with eligible items; minimum-spend boundary; expired code; second redemption; local-currency checkout; and subscription purchase if relevant. Save the observed checkout total and compare it with the advertised promise. Where codes are recipient-specific, test sharing and repeat use instead of treating uniqueness as guaranteed prevention.</p>
<h2>Make cross-flow rules enforceable</h2><p>Decide whether an existing offer stays valid when a shopper enters cart recovery. A reminder can refer to that offer without issuing a deeper one. Document which system owns eligibility and how redemption reaches other flows. Define a conservative fallback if sync fails. Recheck immediately before sending; a property set at flow entry can become stale.</p>
<h2>Compare alternatives without assuming the winner</h2><p>Proof-only content, shipping benefits, access, and monetary discounts are different hypotheses. Shipping benefits still cost money. Do not infer willingness to pay from VIP status or repeated browsing alone. Keep customers assigned consistently to comparison groups where feasible, and include a no-incentive group when evaluating incremental contribution.</p>
<h2>Review after returns, not just at dispatch</h2><p>Track eligible recipients, redemptions, contribution per eligible recipient, full-price order share, refund costs, and complaints about invalid codes. Separate new from repeat customers and market mixes before interpreting trends. Retire stale offers and record approval for exceptions. A successful short sale does not establish that a permanent incentive ladder improves long-term purchasing.</p>
<p><a href="/use-cases/black-friday">Black Friday</a> owns the event calendar and stop decisions. <a href="/use-cases/browse-abandonment">Browse follow-up</a> owns research content before an offer. This policy owns incentive eligibility, checkout behavior, and the financial floor.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration checklist</h2>
<p>Use these profiles as starting points. Ask each supplier to demonstrate this workflow with your events, markets, permissions, and failure cases before buying. Listing is not a verified feature comparison or performance ranking; confirm current plans and destination charges directly.</p>
<ul>{comparisonApps.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
